{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" for global shortcuts and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeyAttachment"},{"type":"text","value":" for element-scoped shortcuts. The common global case stays simple, and scoped behavior feels native to Svelte 5."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"logical-keys-and-physical-positions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logical keys and physical positions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a logical binding when the shortcut should follow the character on the active layout. Use a physical binding when it should follow a keyboard position:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Binding"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Identity checked"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+S"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ key: 'S', mod: true }"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logical "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"event.key"},{"type":"text","value":", with conservative code fallback"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+[KeyS]"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ code: 'KeyS', mod: true }"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exact "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"event.code"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Enter"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logical Enter, including numpad Enter"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[Enter]"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[NumpadEnter]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate physical Enter positions"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every physical code uses brackets in strings, including names shared with logical keys such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[Enter]"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[F13]"},{"type":"text","value":". Supported codes are type-safe and available in autocomplete. Do not put a bracketed code in an object's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" field; use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"code"},{"type":"text","value":". A binding has either "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"code"},{"type":"text","value":", never both."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a layout where the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KeyQ"},{"type":"text","value":" position produces "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"A"},{"type":"text","value":" follows that character and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[KeyQ]"},{"type":"text","value":" follows the position. Logical ASCII letters remain layout-aware; conservative physical fallback helps with transformed output such as macOS Option keys. Exact matches take priority over weaker fallbacks among eligible registrations on the same target."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Callbacks expose the same distinction in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context.parsedHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":": check "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parsed.code !== undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" before reading its physical identity. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"formatForDisplay"},{"type":"text","value":" for labels; stored physical strings retain their brackets."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"global-hotkeys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global hotkeys"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  createHotkey('Mod+S', () => {\n    saveDocument()\n  })\n</script>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The callback receives the original "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KeyboardEvent"},{"type":"text","value":" as the first argument and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeyCallbackContext"},{"type":"text","value":" as the second:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Mod+S', (event, context) => {\n  console.log(context.hotkey)\n  console.log(context.parsedHotkey)\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"scoped-hotkeys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scoped hotkeys"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use attachments instead of capturing an element ref just to pass it back into the API."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeyAttachment } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  const closePanel = createHotkeyAttachment('Escape', () => {\n    close()\n  })\n</script>\n\n<div tabindex=\"0\" {@attach closePanel}>Panel content</div>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reactive-inputs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactive inputs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hotkeys can take plain values for static registrations or getter functions when the hotkey or options depend on reactive state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"reactive-enabled","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":" is false, the hotkey stays registered (visible in devtools); only the callback is suppressed."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  let isEditing = $state(false)\n\n  createHotkey(\n    'Mod+S',\n    () => save(),\n    () => ({ enabled: isEditing }),\n  )\n</script>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"reactive-hotkey-values","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactive hotkey values"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  let shortcut = $state('Mod+S')\n\n  createHotkey(\n    () => shortcut,\n    () => save(),\n  )\n</script>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"changing-a-binding","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Changing a binding"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a new logical or physical binding through your framework's normal state mechanism. A recorder result such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alt+[KeyS]"},{"type":"text","value":" can be passed directly to the same registration API. Keep an initial binding in application state if you want a reset button; the library does not need a separate preferences store."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"property-getters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Property getters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Property getters and functions returning an options object are both supported. Read reactive state inside the getter. A plain value such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ enabled: currentValue }"},{"type":"text","value":" captures the value when that object is created."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter reads option properties inside its reactive computation and updates registrations automatically. Callbacks such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRecord"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onCancel"},{"type":"text","value":" remain functions; the adapter does not call them to resolve options. Tracking is shallow; callback bodies and nested objects are not evaluated to discover dependencies. Keep getters free of side effects. Ordinary option changes preserve registration identity. Changing the target moves the registration to that target."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n\timport { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n\tlet enabled = $state(true)\n\tcreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => console.log('Save'), {\n\t\tget enabled() {\n\t\t\treturn enabled\n\t\t},\n\t})\n</script>\n\n<button onclick={() => enabled = !enabled}>Toggle shortcut</button>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"() => ({ enabled })"},{"type":"text","value":". Svelte applies registration changes when its effect runs. The same forms work for attachments, common options, per-definition options, sequences, and recorders. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeysProvider"},{"type":"text","value":" follows replacement "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" objects and getters within defaults."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"default-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set defaults explicitly with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setHotkeysContext"},{"type":"text","value":" when a subtree needs shared behavior:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { setHotkeysContext } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  setHotkeysContext({\n    hotkey: {\n      preventDefault: false,\n      ignoreInputs: false,\n    },\n  })\n</script>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"common-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common options"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"requirereset","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"requireReset"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { requireReset: true })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"ignoreinputs","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ignoreInputs"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('K', () => openSearch())\ncreateHotkey('Enter', () => submit(), { ignoreInputs: false })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"conflictbehavior","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"conflictBehavior"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { conflictBehavior: 'replace' })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"platform","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"platform"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { platform: 'mac' })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"automatic-cleanup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic cleanup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global hotkeys are automatically unregistered when the owning component unmounts. Attachment-based hotkeys clean themselves up when the attached element is removed or when reactive inputs change."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"registering-multiple-hotkeys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registering multiple hotkeys"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you need to register several hotkeys at once, or a dynamic, variable-length list, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeys"},{"type":"text","value":" (plural) for global shortcuts and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeysAttachment"},{"type":"text","value":" for element-scoped shortcuts:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeys } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  createHotkeys([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n    { hotkey: 'Escape', callback: () => close() },\n  ])\n</script>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"common-options-with-per-hotkey-overrides","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common options with per-hotkey overrides"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass shared options as the second argument. Per-definition options override the common ones:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkeys(\n  [\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo(), options: { enabled: false } },\n  ],\n  { preventDefault: true },\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"dynamic-hotkey-lists","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic hotkey lists"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a getter for reactive arrays:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeys } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  let shortcuts = $state([...])\n\n  createHotkeys(\n    () => shortcuts.map((s) => ({\n      hotkey: s.key,\n      callback: s.action,\n    })),\n  )\n</script>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"scoped-multi-hotkeys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scoped multi-hotkeys"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeysAttachment"},{"type":"text","value":" to scope multiple hotkeys to a specific element:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeysAttachment } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  const editorKeys = createHotkeysAttachment([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n  ])\n</script>\n\n<div tabindex=\"0\" {@attach editorKeys}>Editor content</div>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"metadata-name-description-and-group","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Metadata (name, description, and group)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every hotkey registration can carry a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" object with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"description"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":". Metadata never affects hotkey behavior, but it flows through to registrations and devtools, so you can build shortcut palettes and help screens from it."}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document' },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" option is typed as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeyMeta"},{"type":"text","value":", which ships with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"description"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":" fields. You can extend it with additional properties using TypeScript declaration merging:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"declare module '@tanstack/hotkeys' {\n  interface HotkeyMeta {\n    icon?: string\n  }\n}\n\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document', icon: 'floppy', group: 'File' },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group is descriptive metadata, not an execution scope. A shortcuts panel can group live registration views directly. Disabled registrations remain listed; unmounted registrations disappear."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"introspecting-registrations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Introspecting registrations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getHotkeyRegistrations"},{"type":"text","value":" function to get a live view of all hotkey and sequence registrations. This is useful for building shortcut palettes, help dialogs, or devtools."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getHotkeyRegistrations, formatForDisplay } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  const registrations = getHotkeyRegistrations()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <h2>Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>\n  <ul>\n    {#each registrations.hotkeys as reg (reg.hotkey)}\n      <li>\n        <kbd>{formatForDisplay(reg.hotkey)}</kbd>\n        {#if reg.options.meta?.name}\n          <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>\n        {/if}\n        {#if reg.options.meta?.description}\n          <p>{reg.options.meta.description}</p>\n        {/if}\n      </li>\n    {/each}\n  </ul>\n  {#if registrations.sequences.length > 0}\n    <h2>Sequences</h2>\n    <ul>\n      {#each registrations.sequences as reg (reg.sequence.join(' '))}\n        <li>\n          <kbd>{reg.sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')}</kbd>\n          {#if reg.options.meta?.name}\n            <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>\n          {/if}\n        </li>\n      {/each}\n    </ul>\n  {/if}\n</div>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The returned object contains a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hotkeys"},{"type":"text","value":" array with registration objects including the hotkey string, options (including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":"), and enabled state, and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sequences"},{"type":"text","value":" array containing sequence registrations with the same structure."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-hotkey-manager","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hotkey manager"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can always reach for the underlying manager directly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { getHotkeyManager } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getHotkeyManager()\nmanager.isRegistered('Mod+S')\nmanager.getRegistrationCount()","lang":"ts"}],"headings":[{"id":"logical-keys-and-physical-positions","text":"Logical keys and physical positions","level":2},{"id":"global-hotkeys","text":"Global hotkeys","level":2},{"id":"scoped-hotkeys","text":"Scoped hotkeys","level":2},{"id":"reactive-inputs","text":"Reactive inputs","level":2},{"id":"reactive-enabled","text":"Reactive enabled","level":3},{"id":"reactive-hotkey-values","text":"Reactive hotkey values","level":3},{"id":"changing-a-binding","text":"Changing a binding","level":3},{"id":"property-getters","text":"Property getters","level":2},{"id":"default-options","text":"Default options","level":2},{"id":"common-options","text":"Common options","level":2},{"id":"requirereset","text":"requireReset","level":3},{"id":"ignoreinputs","text":"ignoreInputs","level":3},{"id":"conflictbehavior","text":"conflictBehavior","level":3},{"id":"platform","text":"platform","level":3},{"id":"automatic-cleanup","text":"Automatic cleanup","level":2},{"id":"registering-multiple-hotkeys","text":"Registering multiple hotkeys","level":2},{"id":"common-options-with-per-hotkey-overrides","text":"Common options with per-hotkey overrides","level":3},{"id":"dynamic-hotkey-lists","text":"Dynamic hotkey lists","level":3},{"id":"scoped-multi-hotkeys","text":"Scoped multi-hotkeys","level":3},{"id":"metadata-name-description-and-group","text":"Metadata (name, description, and group)","level":2},{"id":"introspecting-registrations","text":"Introspecting registrations","level":2},{"id":"the-hotkey-manager","text":"The hotkey manager","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nUse `createHotkey` for global shortcuts and `createHotkeyAttachment` for element-scoped shortcuts. The common global case stays simple, and scoped behavior feels native to Svelte 5.\n\n## Logical keys and physical positions\n\nUse a logical binding when the shortcut should follow the character on the active layout. Use a physical binding when it should follow a keyboard position:\n\n| Binding | Identity checked |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Mod+S` or `{ key: 'S', mod: true }` | Logical `event.key`, with conservative code fallback |\n| `Mod+[KeyS]` or `{ code: 'KeyS', mod: true }` | Exact `event.code` |\n| `Enter` | Logical Enter, including numpad Enter |\n| `[Enter]` / `[NumpadEnter]` | Separate physical Enter positions |\n\nEvery physical code uses brackets in strings, including names shared with logical keys such as `[Enter]` and `[F13]`. Supported codes are type-safe and available in autocomplete. Do not put a bracketed code in an object's `key` field; use `code`. A binding has either `key` or `code`, never both.\n\nOn a layout where the `KeyQ` position produces `a`, `A` follows that character and `[KeyQ]` follows the position. Logical ASCII letters remain layout-aware; conservative physical fallback helps with transformed output such as macOS Option keys. Exact matches take priority over weaker fallbacks among eligible registrations on the same target.\n\nCallbacks expose the same distinction in `context.parsedHotkey`: check `parsed.code !== undefined` before reading its physical identity. Use `formatForDisplay` for labels; stored physical strings retain their brackets.\n\n## Global hotkeys\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  createHotkey('Mod+S', () => {\n    saveDocument()\n  })\n</script>\n```\n\nThe callback receives the original `KeyboardEvent` as the first argument and a `HotkeyCallbackContext` as the second:\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', (event, context) => {\n  console.log(context.hotkey)\n  console.log(context.parsedHotkey)\n})\n```\n\n## Scoped hotkeys\n\nUse attachments instead of capturing an element ref just to pass it back into the API.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeyAttachment } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  const closePanel = createHotkeyAttachment('Escape', () => {\n    close()\n  })\n</script>\n\n<div tabindex=\"0\" {@attach closePanel}>Panel content</div>\n```\n\n## Reactive inputs\n\nHotkeys can take plain values for static registrations or getter functions when the hotkey or options depend on reactive state.\n\n### Reactive `enabled`\n\nWhen `enabled` is false, the hotkey stays registered (visible in devtools); only the callback is suppressed.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  let isEditing = $state(false)\n\n  createHotkey(\n    'Mod+S',\n    () => save(),\n    () => ({ enabled: isEditing }),\n  )\n</script>\n```\n\n### Reactive hotkey values\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  let shortcut = $state('Mod+S')\n\n  createHotkey(\n    () => shortcut,\n    () => save(),\n  )\n</script>\n```\n\n### Changing a binding\n\nPass a new logical or physical binding through your framework's normal state mechanism. A recorder result such as `Alt+[KeyS]` can be passed directly to the same registration API. Keep an initial binding in application state if you want a reset button; the library does not need a separate preferences store.\n\n## Property getters\n\nProperty getters and functions returning an options object are both supported. Read reactive state inside the getter. A plain value such as `{ enabled: currentValue }` captures the value when that object is created.\n\nThe adapter reads option properties inside its reactive computation and updates registrations automatically. Callbacks such as `onRecord` and `onCancel` remain functions; the adapter does not call them to resolve options. Tracking is shallow; callback bodies and nested objects are not evaluated to discover dependencies. Keep getters free of side effects. Ordinary option changes preserve registration identity. Changing the target moves the registration to that target.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n\timport { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n\tlet enabled = $state(true)\n\tcreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => console.log('Save'), {\n\t\tget enabled() {\n\t\t\treturn enabled\n\t\t},\n\t})\n</script>\n\n<button onclick={() => enabled = !enabled}>Toggle shortcut</button>\n```\n\nYou can also pass `() => ({ enabled })`. Svelte applies registration changes when its effect runs. The same forms work for attachments, common options, per-definition options, sequences, and recorders. `HotkeysProvider` follows replacement `defaultOptions` objects and getters within defaults.\n\n## Default options\n\nSet defaults explicitly with `setHotkeysContext` when a subtree needs shared behavior:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { setHotkeysContext } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  setHotkeysContext({\n    hotkey: {\n      preventDefault: false,\n      ignoreInputs: false,\n    },\n  })\n</script>\n```\n\n## Common options\n\n### `requireReset`\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { requireReset: true })\n```\n\n### `ignoreInputs`\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('K', () => openSearch())\ncreateHotkey('Enter', () => submit(), { ignoreInputs: false })\n```\n\n### `conflictBehavior`\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { conflictBehavior: 'replace' })\n```\n\n### `platform`\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { platform: 'mac' })\n```\n\n## Automatic cleanup\n\nGlobal hotkeys are automatically unregistered when the owning component unmounts. Attachment-based hotkeys clean themselves up when the attached element is removed or when reactive inputs change.\n\n## Registering multiple hotkeys\n\nWhen you need to register several hotkeys at once, or a dynamic, variable-length list, use `createHotkeys` (plural) for global shortcuts and `createHotkeysAttachment` for element-scoped shortcuts:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeys } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  createHotkeys([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n    { hotkey: 'Escape', callback: () => close() },\n  ])\n</script>\n```\n\n### Common options with per-hotkey overrides\n\nPass shared options as the second argument. Per-definition options override the common ones:\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkeys(\n  [\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo(), options: { enabled: false } },\n  ],\n  { preventDefault: true },\n)\n```\n\n### Dynamic hotkey lists\n\nPass a getter for reactive arrays:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeys } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  let shortcuts = $state([...])\n\n  createHotkeys(\n    () => shortcuts.map((s) => ({\n      hotkey: s.key,\n      callback: s.action,\n    })),\n  )\n</script>\n```\n\n### Scoped multi-hotkeys\n\nUse `createHotkeysAttachment` to scope multiple hotkeys to a specific element:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeysAttachment } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  const editorKeys = createHotkeysAttachment([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n  ])\n</script>\n\n<div tabindex=\"0\" {@attach editorKeys}>Editor content</div>\n```\n\n## Metadata (name, description, and group)\n\nEvery hotkey registration can carry a `meta` object with a `name`, `description`, and `group`. Metadata never affects hotkey behavior, but it flows through to registrations and devtools, so you can build shortcut palettes and help screens from it.\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document' },\n})\n```\n\nThe `meta` option is typed as `HotkeyMeta`, which ships with `name`, `description`, and `group` fields. You can extend it with additional properties using TypeScript declaration merging:\n\n```ts\ndeclare module '@tanstack/hotkeys' {\n  interface HotkeyMeta {\n    icon?: string\n  }\n}\n\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document', icon: 'floppy', group: 'File' },\n})\n```\n\nGroup is descriptive metadata, not an execution scope. A shortcuts panel can group live registration views directly. Disabled registrations remain listed; unmounted registrations disappear.\n\n## Introspecting registrations\n\nUse the `getHotkeyRegistrations` function to get a live view of all hotkey and sequence registrations. This is useful for building shortcut palettes, help dialogs, or devtools.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getHotkeyRegistrations, formatForDisplay } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  const registrations = getHotkeyRegistrations()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <h2>Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>\n  <ul>\n    {#each registrations.hotkeys as reg (reg.hotkey)}\n      <li>\n        <kbd>{formatForDisplay(reg.hotkey)}</kbd>\n        {#if reg.options.meta?.name}\n          <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>\n        {/if}\n        {#if reg.options.meta?.description}\n          <p>{reg.options.meta.description}</p>\n        {/if}\n      </li>\n    {/each}\n  </ul>\n  {#if registrations.sequences.length > 0}\n    <h2>Sequences</h2>\n    <ul>\n      {#each registrations.sequences as reg (reg.sequence.join(' '))}\n        <li>\n          <kbd>{reg.sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')}</kbd>\n          {#if reg.options.meta?.name}\n            <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>\n          {/if}\n        </li>\n      {/each}\n    </ul>\n  {/if}\n</div>\n```\n\nThe returned object contains a `hotkeys` array with registration objects including the hotkey string, options (including `meta`), and enabled state, and a `sequences` array containing sequence registrations with the same structure.\n\n## The hotkey manager\n\nYou can always reach for the underlying manager directly:\n\n```ts\nimport { getHotkeyManager } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getHotkeyManager()\nmanager.isRegistered('Mod+S')\nmanager.getRegistrationCount()\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Hotkeys Guide\nid: hotkeys\n---\n\nUse `createHotkey` for global shortcuts and `createHotkeyAttachment` for element-scoped shortcuts. The common global case stays simple, and scoped behavior feels native to Svelte 5.\n\n## Logical keys and physical positions\n\nUse a logical binding when the shortcut should follow the character on the active layout. Use a physical binding when it should follow a keyboard position:\n\n| Binding | Identity checked |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Mod+S` or `{ key: 'S', mod: true }` | Logical `event.key`, with conservative code fallback |\n| `Mod+[KeyS]` or `{ code: 'KeyS', mod: true }` | Exact `event.code` |\n| `Enter` | Logical Enter, including numpad Enter |\n| `[Enter]` / `[NumpadEnter]` | Separate physical Enter positions |\n\nEvery physical code uses brackets in strings, including names shared with logical keys such as `[Enter]` and `[F13]`. Supported codes are type-safe and available in autocomplete. Do not put a bracketed code in an object's `key` field; use `code`. A binding has either `key` or `code`, never both.\n\nOn a layout where the `KeyQ` position produces `a`, `A` follows that character and `[KeyQ]` follows the position. Logical ASCII letters remain layout-aware; conservative physical fallback helps with transformed output such as macOS Option keys. Exact matches take priority over weaker fallbacks among eligible registrations on the same target.\n\nCallbacks expose the same distinction in `context.parsedHotkey`: check `parsed.code !== undefined` before reading its physical identity. Use `formatForDisplay` for labels; stored physical strings retain their brackets.\n\n## Global hotkeys\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  createHotkey('Mod+S', () => {\n    saveDocument()\n  })\n</script>\n```\n\nThe callback receives the original `KeyboardEvent` as the first argument and a `HotkeyCallbackContext` as the second:\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', (event, context) => {\n  console.log(context.hotkey)\n  console.log(context.parsedHotkey)\n})\n```\n\n## Scoped hotkeys\n\nUse attachments instead of capturing an element ref just to pass it back into the API.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeyAttachment } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  const closePanel = createHotkeyAttachment('Escape', () => {\n    close()\n  })\n</script>\n\n<div tabindex=\"0\" {@attach closePanel}>Panel content</div>\n```\n\n## Reactive inputs\n\nHotkeys can take plain values for static registrations or getter functions when the hotkey or options depend on reactive state.\n\n### Reactive `enabled`\n\nWhen `enabled` is false, the hotkey stays registered (visible in devtools); only the callback is suppressed.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  let isEditing = $state(false)\n\n  createHotkey(\n    'Mod+S',\n    () => save(),\n    () => ({ enabled: isEditing }),\n  )\n</script>\n```\n\n### Reactive hotkey values\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  let shortcut = $state('Mod+S')\n\n  createHotkey(\n    () => shortcut,\n    () => save(),\n  )\n</script>\n```\n\n### Changing a binding\n\nPass a new logical or physical binding through your framework's normal state mechanism. A recorder result such as `Alt+[KeyS]` can be passed directly to the same registration API. Keep an initial binding in application state if you want a reset button; the library does not need a separate preferences store.\n\n## Property getters\n\nProperty getters and functions returning an options object are both supported. Read reactive state inside the getter. A plain value such as `{ enabled: currentValue }` captures the value when that object is created.\n\nThe adapter reads option properties inside its reactive computation and updates registrations automatically. Callbacks such as `onRecord` and `onCancel` remain functions; the adapter does not call them to resolve options. Tracking is shallow; callback bodies and nested objects are not evaluated to discover dependencies. Keep getters free of side effects. Ordinary option changes preserve registration identity. Changing the target moves the registration to that target.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n\timport { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n\tlet enabled = $state(true)\n\tcreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => console.log('Save'), {\n\t\tget enabled() {\n\t\t\treturn enabled\n\t\t},\n\t})\n</script>\n\n<button onclick={() => enabled = !enabled}>Toggle shortcut</button>\n```\n\nYou can also pass `() => ({ enabled })`. Svelte applies registration changes when its effect runs. The same forms work for attachments, common options, per-definition options, sequences, and recorders. `HotkeysProvider` follows replacement `defaultOptions` objects and getters within defaults.\n\n## Default options\n\nSet defaults explicitly with `setHotkeysContext` when a subtree needs shared behavior:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { setHotkeysContext } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  setHotkeysContext({\n    hotkey: {\n      preventDefault: false,\n      ignoreInputs: false,\n    },\n  })\n</script>\n```\n\n## Common options\n\n### `requireReset`\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { requireReset: true })\n```\n\n### `ignoreInputs`\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('K', () => openSearch())\ncreateHotkey('Enter', () => submit(), { ignoreInputs: false })\n```\n\n### `conflictBehavior`\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { conflictBehavior: 'replace' })\n```\n\n### `platform`\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { platform: 'mac' })\n```\n\n## Automatic cleanup\n\nGlobal hotkeys are automatically unregistered when the owning component unmounts. Attachment-based hotkeys clean themselves up when the attached element is removed or when reactive inputs change.\n\n## Registering multiple hotkeys\n\nWhen you need to register several hotkeys at once, or a dynamic, variable-length list, use `createHotkeys` (plural) for global shortcuts and `createHotkeysAttachment` for element-scoped shortcuts:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeys } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  createHotkeys([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n    { hotkey: 'Escape', callback: () => close() },\n  ])\n</script>\n```\n\n### Common options with per-hotkey overrides\n\nPass shared options as the second argument. Per-definition options override the common ones:\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkeys(\n  [\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo(), options: { enabled: false } },\n  ],\n  { preventDefault: true },\n)\n```\n\n### Dynamic hotkey lists\n\nPass a getter for reactive arrays:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeys } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  let shortcuts = $state([...])\n\n  createHotkeys(\n    () => shortcuts.map((s) => ({\n      hotkey: s.key,\n      callback: s.action,\n    })),\n  )\n</script>\n```\n\n### Scoped multi-hotkeys\n\nUse `createHotkeysAttachment` to scope multiple hotkeys to a specific element:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createHotkeysAttachment } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  const editorKeys = createHotkeysAttachment([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n  ])\n</script>\n\n<div tabindex=\"0\" {@attach editorKeys}>Editor content</div>\n```\n\n## Metadata (name, description, and group)\n\nEvery hotkey registration can carry a `meta` object with a `name`, `description`, and `group`. Metadata never affects hotkey behavior, but it flows through to registrations and devtools, so you can build shortcut palettes and help screens from it.\n\n```ts\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document' },\n})\n```\n\nThe `meta` option is typed as `HotkeyMeta`, which ships with `name`, `description`, and `group` fields. You can extend it with additional properties using TypeScript declaration merging:\n\n```ts\ndeclare module '@tanstack/hotkeys' {\n  interface HotkeyMeta {\n    icon?: string\n  }\n}\n\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document', icon: 'floppy', group: 'File' },\n})\n```\n\nGroup is descriptive metadata, not an execution scope. A shortcuts panel can group live registration views directly. Disabled registrations remain listed; unmounted registrations disappear.\n\n## Introspecting registrations\n\nUse the `getHotkeyRegistrations` function to get a live view of all hotkey and sequence registrations. This is useful for building shortcut palettes, help dialogs, or devtools.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { getHotkeyRegistrations, formatForDisplay } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\n  const registrations = getHotkeyRegistrations()\n</script>\n\n<div>\n  <h2>Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>\n  <ul>\n    {#each registrations.hotkeys as reg (reg.hotkey)}\n      <li>\n        <kbd>{formatForDisplay(reg.hotkey)}</kbd>\n        {#if reg.options.meta?.name}\n          <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>\n        {/if}\n        {#if reg.options.meta?.description}\n          <p>{reg.options.meta.description}</p>\n        {/if}\n      </li>\n    {/each}\n  </ul>\n  {#if registrations.sequences.length > 0}\n    <h2>Sequences</h2>\n    <ul>\n      {#each registrations.sequences as reg (reg.sequence.join(' '))}\n        <li>\n          <kbd>{reg.sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')}</kbd>\n          {#if reg.options.meta?.name}\n            <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>\n          {/if}\n        </li>\n      {/each}\n    </ul>\n  {/if}\n</div>\n```\n\nThe returned object contains a `hotkeys` array with registration objects including the hotkey string, options (including `meta`), and enabled state, and a `sequences` array containing sequence registrations with the same structure.\n\n## The hotkey manager\n\nYou can always reach for the underlying manager directly:\n\n```ts\nimport { getHotkeyManager } from '@tanstack/svelte-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getHotkeyManager()\nmanager.isRegistered('Mod+S')\nmanager.getRegistrationCount()\n```\n"}
